import { CorrectionCopy } from "../../correction-model"

pub fn copy -> CorrectionCopy = CorrectionCopy {
  summary: "Iterator names a value from which you can pull an element and its continuation. Pulling leaves the original position unchanged and returns the rest as another Iterator.",
  taskTitle: "Read the rest of a sequence while retaining the original position",
  purpose: "Suppose you want to read a countdown of 3, 2, 1 as each number is needed. Iterator<Int> names a value from which you can pull integers. It retains its position, so you can read the first element twice from the same value. To move forward, use the separate Iterator returned for the rest.",
  setup: "import * as iterator from \"std/iterator\" makes this type and its operations available under the name iterator. let values: iterator.Iterator<Int> = ... names a value values and checks it against the type after the colon. Int means an integer, and <Int> chooses the element type. Iterator<Int> in the function parameters and iterator.Iterator<Int> here are two names for the same type.",
  codeTitle: "Read one position twice, then follow the returned continuation",
  outputTitle: "Seseragi output",
  after: [
    "fn countdown remaining: Int -> Maybe<(Int, Int)> = ... declares the function that calculates one step. The name follows fn; remaining names its argument, the colon gives its type, and -> introduces the return type. The expression after = supplies the returned value. Maybe names two possible shapes: Just carrying a value, or Nothing carrying none. Here Just carries an (Int, Int), a pair of integers in a specified order.",
    "if remaining > 0 then ... else ... chooses the then expression for a positive remaining count, or the else expression otherwise. In Just (remaining, remaining - 1), the first number is the element to return now; the second is the state to use for the following step. At zero, Nothing signals the end. iterator.unfold countdown 3 constructs an Iterator from this function and the starting state 3. Spaces separate the arguments. Construction does not call countdown yet.",
    "first and second each take an Iterator<Int> parameter named values and return String, the text type. iterator.next values returns Just (value, rest) for an element, or Nothing at the end. value is this step's integer; rest is a separate Iterator for the continuation. countdown's second component was an integer state; next's second component already packages the remaining computation as an Iterator.",
    "match chooses the branch matching the returned shape. Nothing -> \"done\" returns an end label. In first, Just (value, _) -> show value names the first component value and turns it into text; _ ignores the second component. In second, Just (_, rest) -> first rest ignores the current element and reads the first element from rest. Every branch returns text. Parentheses around a call group its result as one argument to another call.",
    "The first two lines are 3 and 3 because calling first values does not change values. second values follows the returned rest, producing 2. An Iterator starting at zero is already finished, so the last line is done. This example computes requested elements rather than first constructing a whole Array or List."
  ],
  comparisonTitle: "Compare a moving TypeScript iterator with retained positions",
  comparisonBefore: "In TypeScript, function* declares a generator and yield produces one element at a time. Its Iterator<number> return type supports reading the next element with .next(). Two .next() calls on the same iterator advance its position, giving 3 then 2. That differs from Seseragi's 3 then 3. The program below uses separate position values rather than a JavaScript Iterator to produce the same 3, 3 result as the Seseragi program.",
  comparisonAfter: "The retained-position TypeScript example names its position type Countdown, an object with a remaining field holding the count. Pull names the result type. In next's return type Pull | undefined, | permits either side. { value, rest } returns this number in the value field and the next position in the rest field. End of input returns undefined. Checking for undefined lets the caller read both fields only when a result exists. Reading the same original position twice gives 3, 3; reading its rest gives 2; starting at zero gives done. readonly prevents writing the field through that type; it does not freeze the object at runtime. This small model is specific to the countdown task, not a proposal for a general-purpose iterator library.",
  detailTitle: "Positions, termination, and when computation happens",
  details: [
    "Iterator<Int> promises that any element you pull is an Int. An already-ended Iterator has this type too. It does not specify the number of elements or promise a finite sequence. Collecting every element of an unbounded Iterator into an Array, for example, may never finish.",
    "Calling next again on the same Iterator returns the same result without consuming the original position. This is not a promise to cache and reuse a previously calculated result. For this unfold, every next call invokes countdown once. Reading the original value again calls the step function again.",
    "Iterator computations are pure: they cannot conceal input/output, thrown effects, or Promise-based asynchronous work inside a pull. Here the step only takes a number and chooses the next number. Console output belongs to println in main.",
    "let values: iterator.Iterator<Int> = [1, 2] produces SES-T0101. Annotating an Array as Iterator does not convert it. Use unfold to supply the rule for producing the next element, as here, or an appropriate collection-to-Iterator conversion. The source collection and a value describing how to pull its continuation are different values.",
    "The _ in the exact declaration Iterator<_> marks the element type you choose. The label opaque-type means the internal representation is private. Its canonical identity is std/prelude::Iterator, while this page and its operations belong under std/iterator. No conversion is needed between iterator.Iterator<Int> and Iterator<Int>. The TypeScript comparison uses small integers, not an equivalence between all number values and Int."
  ]
}
